# The set of the nodes reachable from a start node, written as a set.
#
# A family of the reduction series, a graph problem written by its
# definition with sets: a typical problem of informatics contests, written
# definitionally, should run as fast as native code through a common
# standard library, set expressions included, since they express many graph
# problems definitionally.
#
# THE PROGRAM is the definition of the reachable set with a step budget
# (ReachableSet.reachableSetTerm, MergeGraph.budgetedReachBody in
# gezi/contest-corpora):
#
#     reach d v = v + d top (\p. walk p v)
#     walk p v  = the choice of reach p u over the successors u of v
#                 (top for a node without successors)
#
# a node is the Scott unary numeral of its index, let bound once, top is
# corpora/library/top, and the program is reach n s for the node count n and
# the start s. Without the budget a walk round a cycle reads ⊥ in DdLP96's
# reading (a choice is a meet and Y the least fixed point), the whole choice
# with it, and every closed βη normal form would be an answer; with it the
# recursion terminates. Read as written the walks are exponentially many;
# a reading that remembers the nodes it has reached reads the set in time
# linear in the graph.
#
# This family asks for the SET itself, against a snapshot of the reachable
# nodes; the empty set reachability family asks whether one target is
# reachable, a Boolean. The points are distinct programs.
#
# THE GRAPHS are the two rings and the ring with chords (MergeGraph.graph).
# THE NEAR MISS, a proved trap, is the two rings with the bridge from ring A
# to ring B removed (EmptySetReachability.cutGraph): its set is ring A's
# nodes only, so an answer naming a node of ring B is a false answer.
#
# THE SNAPSHOT is the set of the reachable nodes' numerals
# (ReachableSet.reachableNodes, breadth-first search). PROOF, under
# Definition 14 (doc/proofs/reduction_series_answers.md): top ≃_O 0, so
# the program has the answers of the one with 0 in top's place, whose
# reductions terminate (the budget falls at every walk). Its answers are
# then those of its selections, the terms got by choosing one member at
# every choice (the selection theorem), and a choice holding 0 offers no
# member.
# - (⊇) For a node t reachable from s by a path s = v0 -> ... -> vk = t with
#   k < n: choose the walk at v0, ..., v(k-1), each time the successor
#   v(i+1), and at vk the node itself; the selection reduces to the numeral
#   of t.
# - (⊆) A selection either chooses a node at some step, and reduces to the
#   numeral of a node reached from s by the walk chosen so far, or chooses
#   walks until the budget is spent or a node has no successor, where it
#   meets 0 and is no selection. So every answer is the
#   numeral of a reachable node.
# - The near miss: from node 0 of the two rings without the bridge, every
#   edge but the bridge stays in its ring, so the reachable nodes are ring A's.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - breadth-first or depth-first search with a set of visited nodes, and the
#   connected components: J. Hopcroft, R. Tarjan, Algorithm 447: efficient
#   algorithms for graph manipulation, CACM 1973, doi:10.1145/362248.362272;
# - the semi-naive evaluation of recursive queries, which derives each fact
#   once: F. Bancilhon, R. Ramakrishnan, An amateur's introduction to
#   recursive query processing strategies, SIGMOD 1986,
#   doi:10.1145/16856.16859; and tabled evaluation: W. Chen, D. S. Warren,
#   Tabled Evaluation with Delaying for General Logic Programs, J. ACM 1996,
#   doi:10.1145/227595.227597.
#
# READINGS AND SIZES, on the budgeted program (a probe of
# 2026-11-12, a scratch test not committed, contest-framework.slice, CPUs 0
# and 1, load about 6; the steps of `NormalForms.normalForms` within
# 20,000,000, the family's 10 s; the size is the graph's parameter, two
# rings of 12 having 24 nodes): two rings of 12, weak_head 10,348 steps and
# tabling(weak_head) 6,528; of 24, 37,792 and 24,528; of 48, 144,520 and
# 95,088; of 64, weak_head 254,072, tabling out of tables at 115,351: on
# the two rings, whose nodes have one successor each, weak_head's steps grow
# about as the square of the nodes. The ring with chords of 24, weak_head out of
# steps, tabling 15,852; of 48, weak_head out of steps, tabling 99,068; of
# 96 and of 128, both stopped (tabling out of tables at about 128,000). The
# near miss of 12, 4,362 and 2,424, the 12 nodes of ring A. The sizes are
# kept: each class has a point the baseline readers read and one they do
# not (2026-11-12).
# LIMITS: the reduction family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.

#   THE POINTS: reachable_set_point(?Point, ?Graph), sized by the
#   readings above.
#
# The graph is a constructor of reachable_set/graph, whole or cut, whose
# shape is a constructor of schema/graph_shape, two_rings or chord_ring.
# Each other text is from_cbor of a string literal, a Scott list
# of bytes, imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. Each number is from_cbor of an
# integer literal, a Scott integer, imported from corpora/library/integer/cbor.
# FIXME: the imports project "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
